what is this site about  what do we propose here  we propose nothing less  than the total overhaul  of the scientific method  we argue that science itself has been  misconceived misconstrued  from the start  first by the ancient greeks  and then by the generation  that kick-started the 17th century  so-called  scientific revolution  time to take a deep breath now  [Music]  the university of berkeley understanding  science site synthesizes for us in  notions of science that we ended up with  today  since the scientific revolution of the  17th century  essentially science is both a body of  knowledge and an ongoing process of  discovery and science is useful because  it can help us develop new technologies  and yet this prominent university site  lists what causes gravity as one of the  questions that no one using the  contemporary scientific method has been  able to answer in the last 400 years  after 400 years  now how difficult can this be  how is this possible  how is it possible that no think tank on  the planet can be able to figure this  kindergarten stuff out  i mean what do we have  we have the earth  and we have the moon  two rocks  and no one can tell you  why the moon remains faithful to our  planet and doesn't spontaneously fly  away  out of the solar system  so if contemporary so-called science  cannot answer such a simple question  after 400 years maybe there's something  wrong with the notion of science that we  have  maybe the scientific method that we've  been using  is not the one that conduces to such  answers  is science a body of knowledge  is it an ongoing process of discovery  is science supposed to be useful  is the purpose of science to develop new  technologies  does science have anything at all to do  with technology does science have  anything to do with math  the layman seeks something else  they don't care about equations what  they want to understand is the  mechanisms that underlie gravity  magnetism and other such invisible  phenomena  does the current scientific method meet  those expectations  we're going to argue here  that it's time for a major paradigm  shift  [Music]  you
 

